---
title: Remotes
summary: remotes(site) creates nav, meta, page and search as Kit remotes, prerendered by default and live when you ask. The doc body is baked into a region ticket, so the corpus stays on the server.
related:
  - content/site
  - content/search
  - content/collections
---

The site value (`docs` on this page) holds the corpus, so it is server-only, yet the page still needs its data. `remotes()` creates the crossing: the sidebar tree, the shell bundle, the view of one page, and search, as Kit remotes in a `.remote.ts`. The browser imports only these remotes, and the collection never comes along with them.

Every `site()` site needs them, because they are the one supported path from the server-side `site` to a page. Rendering the nav or a body without them would mean importing the collection into a client module, which is exactly the leak the [server-module rule](/docs/content/collections) exists to prevent.

## Create them

```ts title=src/lib/site.remote.ts
import { remotes } from '@ogygia/content/server';
import { docs } from './site.server';

export const { nav, meta, page, search } = remotes(docs, { base: '/docs' });
```

This gives you four remotes, each a plain async function that the page awaits:

- **`nav(slug?)`** returns the sidebar tree with hrefs resolved for `base`. On a [dimensioned](/docs/content/dimensions) site, pass the current slug (or a bare coordinate prefix such as `'v1/'`) so that the tree reflects that coordinate.
- **`meta(slug?)`** returns the full shell bundle `{ nav, switcher, data }` in **one** prerendered call. Pass it straight to `<DocsShell {meta}>`, and the layout never imports the corpus.
- **`page(slug)`** returns the view of one page: `data`, `meta`, `headings`, prev/next, and the **baked body**.
- **`search(q)`** returns ranked hits from [search](/docs/content/search).

::: tip Prefer `meta` over `nav` for shells
`nav` exists for custom chrome that needs only the tree. A shell needs the tree, the switcher and the site facts, and `meta` delivers all three in one prerendered payload, which means one await and one cache entry.
:::

## The body crosses as a ticket

A body is a live [region](/docs/regions/held-regions) rendered in the same SSR pass, so it cannot be serialized as it is. `page` awaits it, which bakes its SSR HTML into the region ticket (HTML only, with no source and no corpus), and the page renders the ticket with `<Region>` through `<Doc>`. [Content collections](/docs/content/collections) explains the baking in more detail.

```svelte
<script>
	import { Doc } from '@ogygia/content';
	import * as docs from '$lib/site.remote';

	const view = (await docs.page(slug))!;   // view.entry.body is a baked ticket
</script>

<Doc {view} />
```

This is why the docs corpus can be megabytes while each page ships only its own baked HTML.

## Prerender or live

By default `nav`, `meta` and `page` **prerender**, meaning they are static payloads computed at build time. Some sites need them to be dynamic, for an authenticated preview or content that varies per request, and the `modes` option sets this per remote:

::: code-group

```ts [static docs (default)]
export const { nav, meta, page, search } = remotes(docs, { base: '/docs' });
// nav/meta/page are baked at build and served static; search runs per request
```

```ts [live preview site]
export const { nav, meta, page, search } = remotes(docs, {
	base: '/docs',
	modes: { page: 'query', nav: 'query' }   // per-request; the site's `context` gates what renders
});
```

:::

- **`'prerender'`** (the default for `nav` / `meta` / `page`) bakes the payload at build time and serves it statically. It is required when the pages that await it are prerendered.
- **`'query'`** runs on the server for each request, and pairs with `site({ context })` for previews and roles.

`search` is always a `query`, since a search is dynamic by nature. For a fully static search box, ship the [index](/docs/content/search) instead and query it in a client worker, in which case you don't create `search` at all.

## Prerender entries

The route still needs to know which slugs to bake. `docs.entries()` returns every leaf in the outline, including declared old addresses, so redirect stubs are baked too:

```ts title=src/routes/docs/[...slug]/+page.server.ts
import { docs } from '$lib/site.server';

export const prerender = true;
export const entries = docs.entries;
export const load = docs.load;   // the 404 guard + alias redirects + per-page checks
```

`docs.load` is the piece people tend to forget. It turns an unknown slug into a real 404 rather than an empty page, serves a **baked 308** for every address an entry declares in `redirect_from`, and runs the per-page [checks](/docs/content/checks) in dev.

## Do / don't

- **Do** create remotes in a `.remote.ts` and import only those from pages, which is all it takes to keep the corpus safe.
- **Do** let `page` bake the body, because the `await` is what turns a live region into something that can cross the wire.
- **Don't** return a raw `entry.body` from a remote or a server load. It is a same-pass render, and ogygia stops you with an error that says so.
- **Don't** use `query` mode by default. Prerendered pages are faster and cheaper, so switch a remote to `query` only when its content really varies per request.
